1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,468
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,468 --> 00:00:08,022
In this short demo, you'll see Azure API Management working as an AI gateway, in front of Microsoft Foundry models and MCP tools.

3
00:00:08,022 --> 00:00:09,134
Everything is live.

4
00:00:09,134 --> 00:00:15,575
The environment was deployed with Bicep, from a GitHub Actions workflow, in Sweden Central and France Central.

5
00:00:16,475 --> 00:00:17,647
Here is the setup.

6
00:00:17,647 --> 00:00:22,401
Apps and agents call one endpoint, on an API Management Basic v2 gateway.

7
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:31,192
Every request goes through a policy chain: managed identity authentication, content safety, a token budget per team, and token metrics.

8
00:00:31,192 --> 00:00:40,373
A load-balanced backend pool then routes it to GPT 6.1 Sol, deployed in two Microsoft Foundry projects, in Sweden Central and France Central.

9
00:00:40,373 --> 00:00:45,062
The same gateway also exposes our Zava retail REST API as an MCP server.

10
00:00:45,962 --> 00:00:50,315
Demo one: a chat completion through the gateway, with the standard OpenAI SDK.

11
00:00:50,315 --> 00:00:54,780
The application only holds an API Management subscription key for the Gold team.

12
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:56,510
There is no model key anywhere.

13
00:00:56,510 --> 00:01:04,492
The gateway authenticates to Microsoft Foundry with its managed identity, and key authentication is actually disabled on the Foundry resources.

14
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:12,027
The response headers show which regional backend served the call, the tokens used, and the tokens left in the team's per-minute budget.

15
00:01:12,927 --> 00:01:14,241
Demo two: resiliency.

16
00:01:14,241 --> 00:01:20,689
Six requests are spread across the backend pool, alternating between Sweden Central and France Central.

17
00:01:20,689 --> 00:01:22,879
Each backend has a circuit breaker.

18
00:01:22,879 --> 00:01:32,394
If a region starts returning 429 or server errors, the gateway trips it for a minute, honors Retry-After, and retries the request on the healthy region.

19
00:01:32,394 --> 00:01:33,959
The client never notices.

20
00:01:34,859 --> 00:01:36,378
Demo three: cost control.

21
00:01:36,378 --> 00:01:40,693
The Bronze marketing sandbox gets only three hundred tokens per minute.

22
00:01:40,693 --> 00:01:48,228
Watch the remaining tokens drop with each call, until the gateway answers 429, Too Many Requests, with a Retry-After header.

23
00:01:48,228 --> 00:01:52,603
At the very same moment, the Gold customer support team is still served.

24
00:01:52,603 --> 00:01:58,255
Each product also carries a monthly token quota, enforced by the same LLM token limit policy.

25
00:01:59,155 --> 00:02:00,188
Demo four: safety.

26
00:02:00,188 --> 00:02:01,966
A normal question goes through.

27
00:02:01,966 --> 00:02:13,212
But a jailbreak attempt, asking the model to ignore its instructions and reveal customer data, is blocked by Prompt Shields at the gateway, with a 403, before a single token is spent on the model.

28
00:02:13,212 --> 00:02:20,499
Hate, self-harm, sexual and violence categories are checked the same way, for every app, without changing any application code.

29
00:02:21,399 --> 00:02:22,362
Demo five: MCP.

30
00:02:22,362 --> 00:02:29,041
Our existing Zava retail REST API was turned into an MCP server by API Management, without writing code.

31
00:02:29,041 --> 00:02:35,912
An MCP client initializes a session, lists two tools, search products and get order status, and calls them.

32
00:02:35,912 --> 00:02:44,260
Because the MCP server is an API in the gateway, it gets the same subscription keys, rate limits and telemetry as everything else.

33
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:46,321
Demo six: an agent.

34
00:02:46,321 --> 00:02:51,577
We ask which outdoor products cost less than one hundred euros, and where my order is.

35
00:02:51,577 --> 00:02:53,594
The agent uses the Responses API.

36
00:02:53,594 --> 00:03:01,171
GPT 6.1 Sol, reached through the gateway, decides to call both MCP tools, also through the gateway, and composes the answer.

37
00:03:01,171 --> 00:03:06,060
The conversation is stateless, so each turn can be served by a different region.

38
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:09,544
Model traffic and tool traffic are governed in one place.

39
00:03:10,444 --> 00:03:11,981
Demo seven: observability.

40
00:03:11,981 --> 00:03:23,271
The LLM emit token metric policy publishes prompt, completion and total tokens to Application Insights, with the product as a dimension, so you can show back or charge back AI costs per team.

41
00:03:23,271 --> 00:03:28,768
LLM logging stores prompts and completions in Log Analytics, ready for audits and dashboards.

42
00:03:29,668 --> 00:03:43,515
That's the AI gateway: keyless security, token budgets, content safety, resilient load balancing, MCP and agent governance, and token-level observability, starting at about one hundred fifty dollars a month with Basic v2.

43
00:03:43,515 --> 00:03:51,912
The Bicep, the GitHub Actions workflows and the demo client are in the repository, and the AI Gateway labs on GitHub take you further.

44
00:03:51,912 --> 00:03:53,165
Thanks for watching.

